      "source_number": "168",
      "task": "Determine whether each of six supplied expressions is already standard form; normalize every expression and state its quantity order.",
      "solution": "In source order: 3.4*10^8 is already standard, n=8; 7.51*10^5 is already standard, n=5; 10.6*10^9=1.06*10^10, not initially standard, n=10; 5.2*10^-4 is already standard, n=-4; 19*10^-6=1.9*10^-5, not initially standard, n=-5; 0.4*10^-8=4*10^-9, not initially standard, n=-9. Dividing a coefficient by 10 increases the exponent by 1; multiplying it by 10 decreases the exponent by 1.",
      "distinction": "Recognize the coefficient interval and compensate exponent changes while preserving value.",
      "response_unit_plan": "One complete six-row classification, normalization and quantity-order result.",
      "limitations": ["Selection does not demonstrate independently writing the six normalized expressions."]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "169",
      "task": "Normalize all nine values, including a mixed number and two fractions, and state all nine quantity orders.",
      "solution": "First six: 7000000000=7*10^9 (9); 25860000000=2.586*10^10 (10); 307920000000=3.0792*10^11 (11); 0.02=2*10^-2 (-2); 0.0013=1.3*10^-3 (-3); 0.000057=5.7*10^-5 (-5). The mixed number 1 1/40 is 41/40=1.025, giving 1.025*10^-5 (-5). (1/6)*10^-7=(5/3)*10^-8=1.666...*10^-8 (-8), with the 6 recurring exactly. 1/700000=(10/7)*10^-6=1.428571428571...*10^-6 (-6), with 428571 recurring exactly.",
      "distinction": "Finite place-value normalization versus exact conversion and normalization of fractional coefficients.",
      "response_unit_plan": "Two mathematical units: one complete table for the six finite decimal/integer values; one complete table for the mixed and fractional values. This is based on distinct conversion procedures, not printed subpart boundaries.",
      "limitations": ["Recognition does not demonstrate producing all nine expressions unaided."]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "170",
      "task": "Rewrite the numbers in all five supplied historical statements, retaining units, the cell-count inequality and both the time and distance numbers in the final statement.",
      "solution": "Area: 510083000=5.10083*10^8 square kilometres. Mass: 6000000000000000000000=6*10^21 tonnes. Cell-count threshold: more than 100000000000000 means more than 1*10^14 cells, not an equality. Hydrogen mass: 0.0000000000000000000000017=1.7*10^-24 grams. Duration: 1/300000=(10/3)*10^-6=3.333...*10^-6 seconds, with 3 recurring exactly. Distance: 1=1*10^0 kilometre. These are provided historical numbers, not verified contemporary scientific assertions.",
      "distinction": "Normalize very large, very small and recurring quantities without changing the inequality, units or historical-data status.",
      "response_unit_plan": "One coherent complete transcription of the six numerical quantities in the five statements.",
      "limitations": ["Selection does not demonstrate independently transcribing the statements."]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "171",
      "task": "Convert kilograms, tonnes and milligrams into grams and normalize each result.",
      "solution": "1 kg=10^3 g, 1 tonne=10^6 g, 1 mg=10^-3 g. Thus 969.5 kg=969500 g=9.695*10^5 g; 43.2 tonnes=43200000 g=4.32*10^7 g; 78 mg=0.078 g=7.8*10^-2 g.",
      "distinction": "Unit scale changes must precede standard-form normalization; conversions act in opposite directions for large and small units.",
      "response_unit_plan": "One complete ordered mass-conversion result.",
      "limitations": ["Recognition does not demonstrate producing conversions unaided."]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "172",
      "task": "Convert 378 square metres into square millimetres and 25 square millimetres into square metres, in standard form.",
      "solution": "1 m=1000 mm implies 1 m^2=10^6 mm^2. Therefore 378 m^2=378*10^6 mm^2=3.78*10^8 mm^2; 25 mm^2=25*10^-6 m^2=2.5*10^-5 m^2.",
      "distinction": "Area conversion squares the length conversion factor and its inverse.",
      "response_unit_plan": "One paired result showing both directions of the same square-unit conversion.",
      "limitations": ["Recognition does not demonstrate writing the unit conversion proof."]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "173",
      "task": "Determine how many places each terminating decimal has after the decimal point.",
      "solution": "Use the finite decimal representation ending at the last nonzero digit, without appended zeros: 3*10^-2=0.03, 2 places; 2.8*10^-2=0.028, 3 places; 1.31*10^-6=0.00000131, 8 places; 5.679*10^-10=0.0000000005679, 13 places. If the coefficient has d decimal places and the power is -k, the resulting count here is d+k.",
      "distinction": "A negative exponent alone does not determine the decimal-place count when the coefficient has fractional digits.",
      "response_unit_plan": "One complete ordered count vector; make the no-trailing-zero convention visible.",
      "limitations": ["The visible convention resolves representational ambiguity; selection does not demonstrate writing the expansions."]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "174",
      "task": "Compute all six products and quotients exactly, with normalized decimal coefficients where standard form is used.",
      "solution": "Product 1: (1.25*4)*10^(6+12)=5*10^18. Quotient 1: (6.2/3.1)*10^(-2-4)=2*10^-6. Product 2: (4.2*5.5)*10^(-3+8)=23.1*10^5=2.31*10^6. Quotient 2: (1/8)*10^(5-(-2))=0.125*10^7=1.25*10^6. Quotient 3: (6.1/2.7)*10^(10-12)=(61/27)*10^-2=2.259259...*10^-2, with 259 recurring exactly. Quotient 4: (3/7)*10^(-15-(-6))=(3/7)*10^-9=(30/7)*10^-10=4.285714285714...*10^-10, with 285714 recurring exactly.",
      "distinction": "Products add exponents; quotients subtract exponents; both may need coefficient normalization, and recurring quotients are not silently rounded.",
      "response_unit_plan": "Two complete operation-family results: both products together, and all four quotients together.",
      "limitations": ["Standard form is an explicit added response condition; selection does not demonstrate independently performing the written calculations."]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "175",
      "task": "Determine four product quantity orders without expanding the full numbers.",
      "solution": "For normalized positive factors, the coefficient product is in [1,100), so the order is the exponent sum, plus 1 exactly when the coefficient product reaches 10. The coefficient products here are 6<10, 4.65<10, 12>=10, 72.8>=10. Exponent sums are -11, 5, 7, 18; adjusted orders are -11, 5, 8, 19. No full decimal product needs to be computed.",
